WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.938
Lesson 1.4: Voice, Ownership, and Ethics in AI Music.

2
00:00:05.938 --> 00:00:09.075
GENAI 101 - Module 1: Music.

3
00:00:09.075 --> 00:00:15.797
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:16.397 --> 00:00:20.537
Think about the song you made in lesson one point one.

5
00:00:20.537 --> 00:00:22.224
Who contributed to it?

6
00:00:22.224 --> 00:00:24.907
You wrote the lyrics and the brief.

7
00:00:24.907 --> 00:00:28.127
The model generated the melody and vocals.

8
00:00:28.127 --> 00:00:35.104
The people whose recordings trained the model contributed
too, whether they knew it or not.

9
00:00:35.104 --> 00:00:38.094
So did the company that built the tool.

10
00:00:38.094 --> 00:00:44.764
Now ask three questions: who should be credited, who should
be paid, and who was asked?

11
00:00:44.764 --> 00:00:47.217
Those questions are this lesson.

12
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:49.025
There are four.

13
00:00:49.025 --> 00:00:51.682
First, training data and consent.

14
00:00:51.682 --> 00:01:01.747
Text-to-music models learned from large collections of
recordings, and in many cases the artists were neither asked
nor paid.

15
00:01:01.747 --> 00:01:03.921
Second, voice and likeness.

16
00:01:03.921 --> 00:01:13.502
Models can imitate a recognizable singer, and several
jurisdictions now treat that as a violation of a person's
rights.

17
00:01:13.502 --> 00:01:19.622
The course rule is simple: prompt for a type of voice, never
a named person.

18
00:01:19.622 --> 00:01:20.991
Third, ownership.

19
00:01:20.991 --> 00:01:32.746
In the United States, the Copyright Office has said that
purely AI-generated work is not protectable, while your own
creative contribution can be.

20
00:01:32.746 --> 00:01:34.276
Fourth, disclosure.

21
00:01:34.276 --> 00:01:39.349
That's why every artifact in this course carries a credit
line.

22
00:01:39.949 --> 00:01:44.156
You'll analyze one real case using a six-step framework.

23
00:01:44.156 --> 00:01:46.109
Identify the stakeholders.

24
00:01:46.109 --> 00:01:48.889
Weigh the harms and benefits to each.

25
00:01:48.889 --> 00:01:51.218
Ask whether consent was sought.

26
00:01:51.218 --> 00:01:54.224
Ask whether the AI's role was disclosed.

27
00:01:54.224 --> 00:01:59.483
Consider whether the same goal could have been reached with
less harm.

28
00:01:59.483 --> 00:02:06.921
Then take a position: what should have happened, and what
rule would you set in your own classroom?

29
00:02:06.921 --> 00:02:09.325
One sentence per step is enough.

30
00:02:09.325 --> 00:02:16.538
Reasonable people will land in different places; what
matters is that your reasoning is visible.

31
00:02:17.138 --> 00:02:24.444
Part of the activity is asking a text model to summarize the
legal and ethical issues in your case.

32
00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:27.248
You'll get a fluent, confident answer.

33
00:02:27.248 --> 00:02:38.908
Then you'll check it against two sources you found yourself,
one news report and one primary source, and mark every claim
as confirmed, unconfirmed, or wrong.

34
00:02:38.908 --> 00:02:44.812
Models blend jurisdictions, go out of date, and occasionally
invent court cases.

35
00:02:44.812 --> 00:02:54.997
Counting what you couldn't confirm is the AI feedback
evaluation competency in action, and it's a habit you'll
want your students to have.

36
00:02:55.597 --> 00:03:06.893
Finally, you'll write an attribution statement for your own
song, naming the human and the machine contributions and
disclosing that AI-generated material is included.

37
00:03:06.893 --> 00:03:09.125
A template is on the lesson page.

38
00:03:09.125 --> 00:03:19.136
Post it in the forum with your case analysis, your count of
confirmed and unconfirmed claims, and the rule you'd set for
AI music in your classroom.

39
00:03:19.136 --> 00:03:26.847
Then find two classmates who took a different position and
engage with their reasoning, not just their conclusion.

40
00:03:26.847 --> 00:03:31.650
This is the debate that will follow you into every school
you teach in.
